00:00:51.842 --> 00:00:53.300
PRAKASH KAMATH:
Let me show you how

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.780
to create a large Dataproc
cluster with preemptible nodes

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.840
using the Google Cloud
console with just a command

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.430
line in minutes.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:04.930
First, we'll create the cluster.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:06.880
We'll give the cluster a name.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.980
Then we'll pick a
place to put it.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:15.090
We are in New York,
so we'll pick US-East.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.960
Next, we get to choose
the cluster mode.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.812
And there are three choices.

00:01:23.812 --> 00:01:25.395
For this demo, we'll
go with Standard.

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:32.160
After that, we get to pick
the shape of the master node.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.880
We'll go with 2 vCPUs.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.680
We'll set the primary
disk to 10 GB, which

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.050
happens to be the minimum.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:43.740
Then we get to customize the
shape of the worker nodes.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:47.520
And you'll see, it's really
easy to customize the shape.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.510
And this was really important
for a customer of mine.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:55.470
We'll set the primary
disk size to 10 GB, again.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:57.660
And we're creating a
really large cluster

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.920
with preemptible nodes, so let's
configure the preemptible nodes

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.520
next.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.970
We'll set the number
of nodes to 400,

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:09.889
and the shape of these nodes
will match the primary worker

00:02:09.889 --> 00:02:11.670
nodes.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:15.300
Now, we could create the cluster
right to this user interface,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:21.220
or what we can use is the REST
command that it auto-generates,

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:26.230
or even the gcloud command,
which is also auto-generated.

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:28.600
Now, because I want to
measure the time it takes

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.360
to create this cluster,
I have a little script

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.240
that we'll run
using Cloud Shell.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.190
And that'll create the
cluster for us today.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.410
Now, as this cluster
is being provisioned,

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:41.890
we can actually
track the progress

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:43.630
through this user interface.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:47.800
So we could go up and
click on the Clusters,

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.850
and you'll see about 400 nodes
being provisioned as we speak.

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:55.770
You can click through
and go to the Details.

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.630
Look at the VM Instance
list, and you'll

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:02.260
see all of these preemptible
nodes getting provisioned.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:03.820
Now, a few things
about you using

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:06.040
preemptibles in your cluster--

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:10.830
they help you run very large
clusters at a low total cost.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.240
The machine type of
these preemptible nodes

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:16.690
matches the machine type
of the primary workers.

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:20.140
Now, within a Dataproc
cluster, preemptible instances

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:22.610
are added to a managed
instance group.

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:25.940
And as you know, they can be
reclaimed at any point of time.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:28.120
So these instances
can be re-added back

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:32.020
into the cluster if and
when capacity is available.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:35.330
Also, a few things to
consider about preemptibles,

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:39.880
they're used as processing nodes
only and do not store data.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:41.650
By default,
preemptible nodes are

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:44.410
created with the
smaller of 100 GB

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:47.480
or the size of the primary
node's persistent disk.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.140
And this is, again, used
only for local caching

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:53.140
and is not available for HDFS.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:56.140
Also, you cannot create
a Dataproc cluster with

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:57.840
preemptible-only nodes.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.350
At minimum, a Dataproc cluster
will have two primary nodes.

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:06.280
This ensures that the cluster
will never run out of workers.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:08.230
You can add or
remove preemptibles

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.630
from a cluster as
needed, and even

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:14.080
set the number of preemptibles
in a cluster to 0.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.579
50% or lower of the total number
of worker being preemptible

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:20.620
is a good working ratio.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:23.200
As you can see, the cluster
is now ready for use,

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:24.910
and it only took a
couple of minutes.

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:26.609
And you can even
submit your jobs

00:04:26.609 --> 00:04:27.775
through this user interface.

